ee our humanity  that didn't believe that our stories  mattered jennifer holness is an  award-winning canadian writer and  producer  the 20-year veteran who now sits on the  black academy's board  says a long history of systemic racism  upheld by white industry gatekeepers  have shut out black and eating creatives  what does systemic racism look like  in the industry oh gosh i mean look  the entire industry is permeated with  systemic racism  from jump every place every part of it  we were told that if we wanted to get  more funding  we should make a canadian story that's  systemic because they're saying that  a black canadian with jamaican roots who  wants to tell a story  that develops a broader world that is  not a canadian story  please join me in honoring my little  brother  the lack of opportunity was painfully  evident for stefan at the canadian  screen awards in 2019  where he received the radius award an  honor given to a canadian who's making  waves globally  i was left with sort of a bittersweet  feeling after i left that stage thinking  man you know i may be you know one of a  handful  of black individuals that's that's gonna  be able to stand on this stage tonight  having an organization like the black  academy  support and nourish black talent  in canada heightens profile  creates avenues for more work and  creates a star system because this is  show business  and it's fed by stars  in the months following george floyd's  death with protests and renewed cries  for racial equality  raging around the world companies  including in canada were taking note  pledging their support on social media  to root out anti-black racism within  their industries  people were maybe paying attention for  the first time you know  maybe there was just nothing else to do  but they  finally hurt us looking back now uh  eight months later do you feel that  those promises are being kept or is it  all lip service  now here's the thing i actually think  that change takes time  s